From 47600dc9fa2cabc648b124ff0d0400085200f861 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dario Gabriel Lipicar <lipigl@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 00:19:38 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Add rep2nim: generate nimside sources from a .rep

repc turns a Qt Remote Objects .rep into a C++ source-side class. rep2nim does
the same for nimside, so a single .rep can define an interface shared between a
Nim implementation and any number of C++/QML replicas -- including, usefully, a
Nim object remoted dynamically and consumed through a repc-generated typed
replica.

Output is a complete `qobject:` block with an `include` of the author's module
spliced in ahead of the slots that forward to it. That placement is what lets
plain, pragma-less procs see both the generated type and its generated property
accessors; `qobject:` already passes such procs through untouched.

Two mappings are load-bearing and easy to get wrong by hand, which is much of
the reason to generate rather than write this:

  * .rep `int` is Qt's int, so it maps to `int32`. Nim's `int` is 64-bit and
    would reach the meta object as qlonglong, silently mismatching every
    replica built from the same .rep.

  * READONLY constrains the replica, not the source: the source still needs a
    setter to publish changes. Only CONSTANT yields neither setter nor notify.

Declaration order is preserved, because that is what QtRemoteObjects'
DynamicApiMap derives its property/signal/method indices from.

Scope is class/PROP/SLOT/SIGNAL. POD, MODEL, ENUM, nested CLASS and USE_ENUM are
rejected by name with a line number rather than mis-generated -- ENUM because
nimside does not emit enums into the meta object yet. Replica-side generation
is not implemented.

tests/test_all: 46 OK.
