--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -62,6 +62,55 @@ discard QApplication.exec()
 
 See [`nim-seaqt`](https://github.com/seaqt/nim-seaqt) for more information.
 
+## `rep2nim`
+
+`rep2nim` is to `nimside` what [`repc`](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtremoteobjects-repc.html) is to C++: it turns a [Qt Remote Objects](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtremoteobjects-index.html) `.rep` file into the source-side object, so one `.rep` can define an interface shared between a Nim implementation and any number of C++ or QML replicas.
+
+```sh
+rep2nim counter.rep --stub    # -> counter_rep.nim, counter_impl.nim
+```
+
+Given
+
+```
+class Counter
+{
+    PROP(int count READONLY)
+
+    SLOT(void increment())
+}
+```
+
+it writes a complete `qobject:` block:
+
+```nim
+qobject:
+  type
+    Counter* = ref object of VirtualQObject
+      count {.qproperty(write = true, notify = true).}: int32
+
+  include "counter_impl"
+
+  proc increment*(self: Counter) {.slot.} =
+    self.incrementImpl()
+```
+
+You supply the bodies in `counter_impl.nim`, as ordinary procs with no pragmas — `qobject:` passes anything that is neither a slot nor a signal through untouched, and the `include` splices them in ahead of the slots that call them, so they can see the generated type and its property accessors:
+
+```nim
+proc incrementImpl*(self: Counter) =
+  self.setCount(self.count + 1'i32)
+```
+
+Declaration order is preserved throughout, because that is what the meta object — and therefore anything reading it, including `QtRemoteObjects`' `DynamicApiMap` — keys off.
+
+Two things are worth knowing:
+
+* **`.rep` `int` becomes Nim `int32`, never `int`.** Nim's `int` is 64-bit and would reach the meta object as `qlonglong`, silently mismatching every replica generated from the same `.rep`.
+* **`READONLY` constrains the replica, not the source.** The generated source still gets a setter, since it is what publishes changes; only `CONSTANT` produces neither setter nor notify signal.
+
+`class`, `PROP`, `SLOT` and `SIGNAL` are supported. `POD`, `MODEL`, `ENUM`, nested `CLASS` and `USE_ENUM` are rejected by name rather than quietly dropped — `ENUM` in particular because `nimside` does not yet emit enums into the meta object. Replica-side generation is not implemented.
+
 ## Related projects
 
 `nimside` was inspired by [`nimqml`](https://github.com/filcuc/nimqml) which offers a similar DSL based on [`DOtherSide`](https://github.com/filcuc/dotherside).
